  <!-- SECTION 2: FLINT VS DENT HETEROSIS & DIVERSITY -->
  <section id="flints-heterosis" class="mgdb-panel" aria-labelledby="flints-heterosis-title">
    <div class="flints-panel-header-wrap">
      <div>
        <h2 id="flints-heterosis-title" class="mgdb-panel-title">Flint vs. Dent Heterosis &amp; Cold Climate Adaptation</h2>
        <p class="mgdb-panel-lead">How European flint genomes diverged from American Dent lines to provide cold tolerance and complementary hybrid vigor.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flints-heterosis-grid">
      <div class="flints-heterosis-card">
        <div class="flints-heterosis-icon" style="background:#e0f2fe; color:#0369a1;">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="28" height="28" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle>
            <path d="M12 2a14.5 14.5 0 0 0 0 20 14.5 14.5 0 0 0 0-20"></path>
            <path d="M2 12h20"></path>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <h3>Post-Columbian Introduction &amp; Lineage Divergence</h3>
        <p>
          Following multiple introduction waves from the Caribbean and North America in the 16th century, European maize established a distinct flint germplasm pool. Through centuries of regional selection in Central and Northern Europe, flint landraces adapted to shorter growing seasons and cooler climates, accumulating extensive chromosomal and retrotransposon differences from American Dent germplasm.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flints-heterosis-card">
        <div class="flints-heterosis-icon" style="background:#fef3c7; color:#b45309;">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="28" height="28" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <path d="M12 2v20"></path>
            <path d="M17 5H9.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 0 0 7h5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 0 7H6"></path>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <h3>Genomic Basis of Dent &times; Flint Heterosis</h3>
        <p>
          Hybrid maize breeding in Europe fundamentally exploits the strong heterotic pool complementation between European Flint and US Dent \(such as B73 and Mo17\). Comparative genomics reveals that gene copy number variations, presence/absence variations \(PAVs\), and non-overlapping retrotransposon insertions between flint and dent genomes drive hybrid vigor in F1 progeny.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flints-heterosis-card">
        <div class="flints-heterosis-icon" style="background:#dcfce7; color:#15803d;">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="28" height="28" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <path d="M12 2.69l5.66 5.66a8 8 0 1 1-11.31 0z"></path>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <h3>Cold Tolerance &amp; Early Growth Vigor</h3>
        <p>
          Flint inbreds exhibit superior germination, seedling emergence, and photosynthetic capacity under low spring temperatures \(10–14&deg;C\). Assemblies of DK105, EP1, F7, and PE0075 uncover duplications in gene networks governing cold-stress signaling, chloroplast stability, anthocyanin biosynthesis, and rapid cell division during juvenile growth.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 3: KEY PUBLICATIONS & PREPRINTS -->
  <section id="flints-publications" class="mgdb-panel" aria-labelledby="flints-pubs-title">
    <div class="flints-panel-header-wrap">
      <div>
        <h2 id="flints-pubs-title" class="mgdb-panel-title">Key Publications &amp; Project Literature</h2>
        <p class="mgdb-panel-lead">Foundational peer-reviewed studies and preprints detailing the sequencing, annotation, and structural variations of European flint lines.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flints-pubs-grid">
      <!-- Haberer et al. 2020 -->
      <article class="flints-pub-card">
        <div class="flints-pub-badge-row">
          <span class="flints-pub-badge flints-badge-peer">Peer-Reviewed Article</span>
          <span class="flints-pub-journal">Nature Genetics &bull; September 2020</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="flints-pub-title">
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41588-020-0671-9" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            European maize genomes highlight intraspecies variation in repeat and gene content
          </a>
        </h3>
        <p class="flints-pub-authors">
          Haberer, G., Kamal, N., Bauer, E., Gundlach, H., Fischer, I., Seidel, M. A., Spannagl, M., Marcon, C., Ruban, A., Urbany, C., Nemri, A., Hochmuth, A., Schoof, H., Mayer, K. F. X., &amp; Sch&ouml;n, C.-C.
        </p>
        <div class="flints-pub-meta-row">
          <span><strong>Citation&#58;</strong> <em>Nature Genetics</em> 52, 950–957 \(2020\)</span>
          <span><strong>DOI&#58;</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41588-020-0671-9" target="_blank" rel="noopener">10.1038/s41588-020-0671-9</a></span>
        </div>

        <details class="flints-abstract-drawer">
          <summary class="flints-abstract-toggle">
            <span class="flints-toggle-text">Show Abstract</span>
            <span class="flints-toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true">&darr;</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="flints-abstract-content">
            <p>
              Maize \(<em>Zea mays</em> L.\) is a model plant for genetic studies and a major crop worldwide. Modern hybrid breeding exploits the complementarity between distinct germplasm pools, such as European flint and American dent. Here we report high-quality de novo sequence assemblies of four Central European flint inbred lines \(DK105, EP1, F7, and PE0075\) that capture founders of European hybrid maize. Comparative analyses with US dent reference genomes reveal high intraspecific diversity, with striking variation in transposable element dynamics, a 1.6-fold difference in long terminal repeat retrotransposon turnover rates, and pervasive copy number variation in defense, cold tolerance, and developmental gene families. These assemblies complement the maize pan-genome and provide a mechanistic framework for understanding dent &times; flint heterosis in temperate agriculture.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <div class="flints-pub-actions">
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41588-020-0671-9" class="mgdb-btn mgdb-btn-primary flints-btn-sm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read Paper at Nature Genetics &#8599;</a>
          <a href="/data_center/reference?author=Haberer" class="mgdb-btn mgdb-btn-secondary flints-btn-sm">MaizeGDB Reference Record &rarr;</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- Unterseer et al. 2017 -->
      <article class="flints-pub-card">
        <div class="flints-pub-badge-row">
          <span class="flints-pub-badge flints-badge-preprint">Preprint</span>
          <span class="flints-pub-journal">bioRxiv &bull; January 2017</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="flints-pub-title">
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1101/103747" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            European Flint reference sequences complement the maize pan-genome
          </a>
        </h3>
        <p class="flints-pub-authors">
          Unterseer, S., Pophaly, S. D., Peis, R., Frascaroli, E., Schippery, W., Mayer, K. F. X., Bauer, E., &amp; Sch&ouml;n, C.-C.
        </p>
        <div class="flints-pub-meta-row">
          <span><strong>Repository&#58;</strong> <em>bioRxiv</em> 103747 \(2017\)</span>
          <span><strong>DOI&#58;</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1101/103747" target="_blank" rel="noopener">10.1101/103747</a></span>
        </div>

        <details class="flints-abstract-drawer">
          <summary class="flints-abstract-toggle">
            <span class="flints-toggle-text">Show Abstract</span>
            <span class="flints-toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true">&darr;</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="flints-abstract-content">
            <p>
              Maize was introduced to Europe in the sixteenth century and adapted to climatic conditions distinct from its Central American origin. To expand the genomic resources of the maize pan-genome beyond American dent lines, we assembled BAC-based and whole-genome shotgun sequences of European flint inbreds. These data demonstrate that flint reference genomes uncover novel genes and non-shared structural variants absent in B73, facilitating high-resolution QTL mapping and genomic prediction in European hybrid breeding pipelines.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <div class="flints-pub-actions">
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1101/103747" class="mgdb-btn mgdb-btn-primary flints-btn-sm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read bioRxiv Preprint &#8599;</a>
          <a href="/data_center/reference?author=Unterseer" class="mgdb-btn mgdb-btn-secondary flints-btn-sm">MaizeGDB Reference Record &rarr;</a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 4: DATA HOSTED AT MAIZEGDB -->
